  "result": "The command now prints **27.06**, and all 6 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded a one-digit fraction on the left instead of the right. `\"5\".rjust(2, \"0\")` gives `\"05\"`, so `\"12.5\"` became 1205 cents ($12.05) instead of 1250. That made the quote 2 \u00d7 12.05 = 24.10, plus 8.25% tax = 26.09.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `rjust` to `ljust`, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. Now 2 \u00d7 $12.50 = $25.00, plus $2.06 tax = **27.06**. I also checked a few other inputs and they convert correctly: `12` \u2192 1200, `12.05` \u2192 1205, `0.1` \u2192 10.\n\npytest isn't installed here, so I ran the tests with `python3 -m unittest`. No existing test used a price with a single decimal place, which is how this got through. If you want a regression test for `to_cents(\"12.5\") == 1250`, I can add one.",
